We can compute this by finding areas of smaller triangles.  Let's break it down step-by-step.  First we need some key facts about the given figures.  Since they are coplanar squares, their diagonals bisect each other at right angles. So \[
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Area } \triangle ABE = \frac{1}{2} AB \cdot BE \\
&= \frac{1}{2} (AB) \cdot (BE)
\end{aligned}
\]\[
\begin{aligned}
& \text{Area } \triangle BCF = \frac{1}{2} BC \cdot CF \\
&= \frac{1}{2} (BC) \cdot (CF)
\end{aligned}
\]\[
\begin{aligned}
& \text{Area } \triangle CDE = \frac{1}{2} CD \cdot DE \\
&= \frac{1}{2} (CD) \cdot (DE)
\end{aligned}
\]\[
\begin{aligned}
& \text{Area } \triangle ADE = \frac{1}{2} AD \cdot AE \\
&= \frac{1}{2} (AD) \cdot (AE)
\end{aligned}
\]\[
\begin{aligned}
& \text{Area } \triangle GCE = \frac{1}{2} GC \cdot CE \\
&= \frac{1}{2} (GC) \cdot (CE)
\end{aligned}
\]Thus, \( \text{area}(ABC) = \text{area}(ADE) + \text{area}(GCE) - \text{area}(ABE)\). We know that \( CD = 10 \) and \( AH = 5 \), so \( AD = 15 \) and \( AG = 5 \). Then from the diagram, \( CG = GH = 5 \). Thus, \[
\begin{aligned}
\text{area}(ABC) &= \frac{1}{2} (15)(5) + \frac{1}{2} (5)(5) - \frac{1}{2} (5)(5) \\
&= \boxed{\frac{75}{2}}
\end{aligned}
\]

